    "encrypted": "false",
    "long_instructions": "## Build a Kitchen Basics Bot\r\n\r\nA Cooking Instructor set up this bot to answer questions from beginners — but they weren't careful about what information they gave it.\r\n\r\nThis is supposed to help with topics like handwashing, cooking meat safely, microwave safety, cutting boards, and other cooking basics.\r\n\r\n##### Do This:\r\n\r\n1. Start by asking your bot one big question — something like what a person new to this would want to know overall. Then ask a few follow-ups. Notice where it can't give you a straight answer.\r\n2. Open the Retrieval Contexts panel. That's everything your bot knows. Read through it.\r\n3. Fix the context. You can delete cards or edit them to fix them. Some cards are a judgement call - decide what you would trust.\r\n4. Click Update, then ask your questions again. Keep going until you would trust this bot in the class kitchen.\r\n5. Try asking something your bot has no information about. What does it say now?\r\n\r\n##### Talk with your partner:\r\nCompare bots — did you keep or edit the same things? Does having context mean the bot is always right?\r\n\r\n::: details [**Have Time To Make It Better?**]\r\nAdd any of these to your bot's context, then test whether it helps:\r\n\r\n- [Updated February 2026] Turn pot handles toward the back of the stove so they cannot\r\nbe bumped or grabbed by accident.\r\n- [Updated February 2026] Read the whole recipe before you start cooking, and get all\r\nyour ingredients out first.\r\n- [Updated February 2026] Cut away from your body and keep your fingers curled back,\r\naway from the blade.\r\n\r\n:::\r\n\r\n::: details [**Ready for a challenge?**]\r\nFind one more fact this bot should know. Write it as a new context entry — include the date and where you found it, just like the other cards do. Then test whether your bot uses it.\r\n:::\r\n",
    "aichat_settings": {
      "initialCustomizations": {
        "selectedModelId": "gemini-2.5-flash",
        "temperature": 0.5,
        "systemPrompt": "You are a kitchen helper for a beginner cooking class at the community center. A cooking instructor built you to answer questions from students who are new to cooking.",
        "retrievalContexts": [
          "[Updated February 2026] Wash your hands with soap and warm water for at least 20\nseconds before you start cooking, and again after handling raw meat.",
          "[Updated February 2026] Never put metal in a microwave. Metal can spark and start a\nfire. Use glass or microwave-safe containers instead.",
          "[Updated 2015] Use a separate cutting board for raw meat and for vegetables so that\nbacteria from the meat does not spread. Aluminum foil is safe to use in a microwave as\nlong as it is not crumpled.",
          "[Updated February 2026] The community center also offers a woodworking class on\nSaturday mornings and a pottery class on Wednesday evenings.",
          "Keep a lid nearby when cooking on the stove. If a pan catches fire, covering it with\na lid smothers the flames. Never pour water on a grease fire.",
          "[Updated February 2026] Kitchen safety is important. Students should be careful when\nusing kitchen equipment and follow good practices.",
          "[Updated 2012] Chicken is safe to eat as soon as the outside is browned, even if the\ncenter is still slightly pink.",
          "[Updated February 2026] Raw chicken must be cooked all the way through before eating.\nNever eat chicken that is still pink inside or still has raw juices."
        ],
